.\" hydra-checksum(1)
.TH HYDRA\-CHECKSUM 1 "2026-08-17" "hydra" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
hydra-checksum \- report the digests a server advertises
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hydra checksum
.RI [ options ]
.IR URL ...
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
A checksum is a function of the bytes, so this subcommand cannot compute one
for bytes it has not received.
No protocol lets a client ask a server to hash an object on demand: RFC 9530
defines
.B Want\-Digest
for exactly that, and measurement across four representative hosts found
zero support.
.PP
What
.B "hydra checksum"
does instead is retrieve a digest the
.I publisher
already computed.
That is a different and weaker claim.
It verifies the bytes you eventually download against what the publisher
says they should be, and it lets you compare a local file against a remote
object without transferring it.
It does
.I not
verify the server: a host that serves wrong bytes can serve a matching wrong
digest, unless the digest comes from somewhere else \(em an index, a
signature, another host.
.
.SS Sources, in descending order of trust
.TP
.B 1. Response headers
.BR Repr\-Digest / Content\-Digest
(RFC 9530), the older
.B Digest
(RFC 3230),
.BR Content\-MD5 ,
and the cloud-store forms
.BR x\-amz\-checksum\-sha256 ,
.BR x\-amz\-checksum\-crc32 ,
.BR x\-goog\-hash ,
.BR x\-ms\-blob\-content\-md5 .
These arrive on a HEAD: one round trip, no body.
.TP
.B 2. A sidecar file
.IB name .sha256 ", " .md5 ", " .sha1 ", " CHECKSUMS ", " SHA256SUMS .
Tens of bytes and very common for releases \(em but served by the same host,
so it proves integrity, not authenticity.
.TP
.B 3. An ecosystem index
PyPI's JSON API and the crates.io sparse index publish per-file digests from
a
.I different
endpoint than the artifact, so they carry some authenticity weight.
.
.SS What an ETag is not
An
.B ETag
is an opaque validator.
It is frequently a hash of something, which invites the assumption that it
is a hash of the
.IR content .
It is not, and treating it as one reports false mismatches: measured on a
real host whose ETag is exactly 64 hex characters, it equalled neither the
content SHA-256 nor the git blob id.
A weak ETag
.RB ( W/\(dq...\(dq )
is weaker still, since the specification permits it to compare equal across
representations that are merely
.IR equivalent .
This subcommand therefore prints an ETag labelled as a validator and
explicitly not a content digest, and the JSON carries
.B validator_is_not_a_digest
for the same reason.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-verify " FILE"
Compare
.I FILE
against the advertised digest.
This is the fast way to answer "is my copy current?" without refetching.
The verdict is
.BR MATCH ,
.BR MISMATCH ,
or an explicit statement that nothing comparable was advertised \(em which
is
.I not
reported as a pass.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output.
.TP
.B \-\-sidecars
Also try sidecar and manifest files, not just headers (one extra request
each).
On by default, and see
.BR BUGS .
.TP
.B \-\-download\-if\-needed
Compute the digest by streaming the body when nothing is advertised.
Off by default, because transferring the object is the opposite of what this
subcommand is for; on, it is the honest fallback rather than a silent
absence.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.
.SH OUTPUT
Human output names each digest, its source, and whether the algorithm is
collision-resistant \(em
.B sha256
and
.B sha512
are;
.B sha1
and
.B md5
are flagged
.BR "[not collision-resistant]" .
When nothing is advertised, the output says so and points at
.BR \-\-download\-if\-needed ,
rather than printing nothing and letting silence read as success.
.PP
.B \-\-json
emits
.B objects
(an array) and
.BR ok .
Each object carries
.BR url ,
.BR size ,
.BR validator ,
.BR validator_is_not_a_digest ,
.B advertised
(each with
.BR algo ,
.BR hex ,
.BR source ,
.BR collision_resistant ),
.BR computed_sha256 ,
and
.BR verify .
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every URL was inspected and, if
.B \-\-verify
was given, matched.
.TP
.B 1
A verification mismatched, or a local file could not be read.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ask what a release server advertises:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra checksum https://example.com/release.tar.gz
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check a local copy against the publisher's digest without refetching:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra checksum \-\-verify ./release.tar.gz \e
      https://example.com/release.tar.gz
.fi
.RE
.PP
Machine-readable, falling back to a streamed digest when the server
advertises none:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hydra checksum \-\-json \-\-download\-if\-needed URL | jq '.objects[0]'
.fi
.RE
.
.SH BUGS
.B \-\-sidecars
is on by default and this release provides no way to switch it off.
It is declared as taking no value, so
.B "\-\-sidecars false"
prints a parse complaint about
.BR false ,
then proceeds with sidecars enabled.
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hydra (1),
.BR hydra\-parity (1)
.PP
.IR docs/INTEGRITY.md .
.
.SH STANDARDS
RFC 9530 (digest fields,
.BR Want\-Digest ),
RFC 3230 (the older
.B Digest
header), RFC 9110 (validators and
.BR ETag ).
